#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "44c87021-177d-5f5c-b1f9-47eb08f107a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-4146" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ if $\\ln(4x+5)=3$." :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e$. So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (4x + 5) &= 3 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (4x + 5)} &= e^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln(4x+5)} = 4x+5\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 5 &= e^{3} \\\\\n4x &= e^{3} - 5 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{e^{3} - 5}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "0cfb7d21-6ca4-55a3-a46a-7a765ed1b866" :question/math-academy-id "e-4147" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $3\\ln (5x+1)-2 = 13$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\ln (5x + 1) - 2 &= 13 \\\\\n3\\ln (5x + 1) &= 15 \\\\\n\\ln (5x + 1) &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e$, so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (5x + 1) &= 5 \\\\\ne^{\\ln (5x + 1)} &= e^{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln (5x+1)} = 5x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 1 &= e^{5} \\\\\n5x &= e^{5} - 1 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{e^{5} - 1}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "85f82353-2436-5e48-984a-5e671ee54024" :question/math-academy-id "e-4148" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ such that $\\ln(1-2x) + 5\\ln(1-2x) = 4$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - 2x) + 5\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n(1 + 5)\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n6\\ln (1 - 2x) &= 4 \\\\\n\\ln (1 - 2x) &= \\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the natural logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the natural logarithm is $e$, so we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln (1 - 2x) &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\ne^{\\ln (1 - 2x)} &= e^{2/3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that\n\n$$\ne^{\\ln(1-2x)} = 1-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - 2x &= e^{2/3} \\\\\n1 - 2x &= \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} \\\\\n- 2x &= \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} - 1 \\\\\nx &= -\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} - 1}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{-\\sqrt[3]{e^{2}} + 1}{2} \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1 - \\sqrt[3]{e^{2}}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96def490-ed4b-56b8-8b36-ee466b3ccf6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107495} {:answer/id #uuid "9d50102c-7b29-58f0-aba8-24afc6989461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107496} {:answer/id #uuid "c401e864-b52f-511f-8e0b-b3bad9ac675f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}e^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107497} {:answer/id #uuid "fea031c6-c660-5751-9b80-7a439235cb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}e^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107498} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd7f84d-f682-53e8-9b43-32c4063fa0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{1/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107499}] :answer-field/id #uuid "56827e89-7d4e-5ed2-b123-3e520ac5f54c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107494}] :question/id #uuid "49019dd1-55bb-562f-a7d6-f6148b65a5e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-19175" :question/problem "Find $x$ such that $\\ln (5x) = 5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dec21a9-eec8-5e39-9af6-5ba355a11c5b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "151a655e-4d18-5ef1-b8cd-5631f4184ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-19181" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{3})=3.$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The logarithm is a natural logarithm, which means its base is $e.$ So, we use a base of $e$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{x}{3}) & =3 \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x/3)} & ={e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${e}^{\\operatorname{ln}(x/3)}=\\frac{x}{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & ={e}^{3} \\\\ x & =3{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8c27840-76fa-5c01-98cc-587dc063f19c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8826f4c0-5a42-59a2-9151-b35e6ad20bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109940} {:answer/id #uuid "e2abd7bd-8369-5830-b802-b60a279694c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109941} {:answer/id #uuid "3de17066-fadf-5f18-b644-223597f74318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109942} {:answer/id #uuid "484e37cc-7935-5581-9dee-8d9c9f460038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109943} {:answer/id #uuid "5c270b95-b18d-51d3-bd3a-4c9f0a72b267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109944}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b43822f8-5c74-5fd7-8419-7849b452203b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109939}] :question/id #uuid "4428092c-dd91-56c8-97be-17f43bd92085" :question/math-academy-id "q-66748" :question/problem "If $\\ln x = 1 - 2\\ln x$, then what is the value of $x$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ae88a0b-9927-573b-a072-d0aa1861a9cd" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Natural Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4068a3-24fb-5f47-a09c-2ea9992503c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4} - 3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110108} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6009bf-33f2-5efd-8b0c-6665ae87ab26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7 - e^{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110109} {:answer/id #uuid "f1793b0e-bced-5a97-8060-de84045da96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24 - e^{4}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110110} {:answer/id #uuid "6200a4b2-b960-5d6a-b7d2-b9c6cab0e553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{4} - 24}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110111} {:answer/id #uuid "6a84100c-2290-501c-8d9a-6766d6c44303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 - e^{4}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110112}] :answer-field/id #uuid "90195487-fcb1-57cc-8d0f-33b1c9fdb8ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110107}] :question/id #uuid "bba3adf3-06bf-565a-834f-7b41da6bd21b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7940" :question/problem "Find the exact solution to $4\\ln (3 - 7x) + 8 = 24$."}]}
